# Student LMS Coding Guidelines

## Core Standards
- Use TypeScript strict mode patterns; avoid `any` unless absolutely necessary.
- Reuse existing components, utilities.
- Prefer `shadcn/ui` and Radix UI primitives wherever possible before building custom UI from scratch.
- Prefer icons from a maintained icon library (for example, `@phosphor-icons/react`) over image URLs or custom icon assets wherever possible.
- Keep components focused and readable; extract reusable logic when duplication appears.
- Keep file size minimal; max file length is 200 lines. Split into smaller components/hooks/utils when approaching this limit.
- Handle edge cases explicitly (empty states, invalid inputs, null/undefined values).

## API Architecture
- Expose all backend functionality as REST API routes only (file routes under
  `src/routes/api/**` with `server.handlers` for GET/POST/etc.). Do not add new
  TanStack `createServerFn` server functions for new work.
- Structure each endpoint as route handler → handler (`src/server/api/**/handlers`)
  → service (`src/server/api/**/*.service.ts`), and call it from the client via
  the typed `fetchJson` helpers in `src/lib/api/**`.
- Access the database through the Drizzle query builder (`db.select`, `db.insert`,
  `db.update`, `db.delete` from `@/db`). Avoid raw `sql` template queries unless a
  query genuinely cannot be expressed with the builder.

## Testing Requirements
- Add or update tests for every non-trivial behavior change.
- Prefer behavior-driven tests with Testing Library over implementation details.
- Cover positive path, fallback/edge case, and critical UI state transitions.
- Keep tests deterministic; avoid timing-sensitive assertions where possible.
- Write tests side by side with implementation in the same PR; do not defer tests to follow-up work.
- Use `vitest` for unit tests and keep test files colocated or clearly paired with source files (for example, `foo.ts` + `foo.test.ts`).
- Coverage target for this repo is 100%; at minimum, any touched module must stay at 100% statement/branch/function/line coverage.

## Testing Documentation
- Keep `docs/testing/feature-test-matrix.md` updated for every touched feature.
- Add or update `docs/testing/features/<feature>.md` when tests are added/changed/removed.
- Complete `docs/testing/pr-checklist.md` in every PR that changes feature behavior or test setup.
- Ensure documented test commands are valid and runnable.

## Styling and UX
- Follow existing Tailwind and component style conventions in the same feature area.
- Avoid hardcoded magic values when a project token or shared constant exists.
- Preserve accessibility basics (semantic elements, interactive affordances, readable text).

## Design & Motion System ("the delightful feel")
The product's bar is not "functional" — it is **delightful**. Every screen should
feel premium and alive: each hover, tap, and load should have a small, tasteful
reaction so the UI feels responsive and "wow" without ever being noisy or slow.
Match this feel in all new and touched UI. Restraint is part of the bar — a few
well-placed motions beat many competing ones.

### Reusable motion kit (use these first)
A shared animation kit lives in `src/styles.css` — **reuse these classes; do not
reinvent per-component keyframes or add new global keyframes without need.**
- `animate-dash-rise` — section/panel entrance (fade + rise + blur-to-sharp).
- `animate-dash-row-in` — list-row / item entrance.
- `dash-lift` — hover lift + press-squish for clickable cards/rows.
- `dash-sheen` — one-shot sheen sweep on hover (sets `position:relative` +
  `overflow:hidden`; do **not** put it on containers whose children must overflow,
  e.g. absolutely-positioned badges).
- `dash-skeleton` — shimmer skeleton block for loading (prefer over static gray
  pulses / spinners for content placeholders).
- `animate-dash-pop` — springy pop-in for count badges / newly-shown icons.
- `animate-dash-glow` — breathing accent glow for "today"/live emphasis.
- `animate-dash-ping` — expanding ring behind a live dot.
- `animate-dash-float` — gentle idle float for illustrations/empty-state icons.
- `dash-gradient-text` — animated indigo→violet gradient text; use sparingly
  (greetings/hero only, never body copy).
- Stagger entrances with the `--dash-delay` CSS variable — either the
  `[--dash-delay:0.08s]` utility or an inline style cast as
  `style={{ '--dash-delay': `${i * 0.05}s` } as React.CSSProperties}`. Cap
  cumulative list delay (~0.4s, e.g. `Math.min(i, 8) * 0.05`) so long lists stay
  snappy.

### Interaction conventions (apply consistently)
- **Clickable cards/rows:** `dash-lift` (or hover lift + `active:scale-[0.98]`)
  plus an accent border tint on hover (`hover:border-[#4F6BED]/35`).
- **Buttons / CTAs:** hover lift (`hover:-translate-y-px`) + press
  (`active:scale-95`); primary CTAs get an accent glow shadow. Use smooth
  `transition-*` with `duration-150`–`300` and `ease-out`.
- **Icons in interactive elements:** nudge/scale on hover (chevrons slide with
  `group-hover:translate-x-1`, toggle chevrons rotate `data-[state=open]:rotate-180`,
  decorative icons `group-hover:scale-110`).
- **Overlays (dropdown / select / menu / modal / drawer / tooltip):** must
  animate open **and** close. The shared primitives in `src/components/ui`
  (`dropdown-menu`, `select`, `modal`, `sheet`, `tooltip`,
  `masai-dropdown-checkbox-filter`, `masai-drawer`) already encode this — reuse
  them. Standard timing: `data-[state=open]:duration-200 ease-out`,
  `data-[state=closed]:duration-150`, entering with zoom/slide + fade from the
  trigger origin. New menu/filter items get `transition-colors` + a press
  response.
- **Loading:** `dash-skeleton` shimmer that mirrors the real layout; keep the
  literal "Loading…" text as `sr-only` when a test or screen reader needs it.
- **Empty states:** make them friendly, not blank — a popping/floating icon
  (`animate-dash-pop` / `animate-dash-float`) with a short line. Preserve the
  exact `data-testid` and copy tests assert on.

### Brand accents
- Indigo `#4F6BED`, purple `#6962AC`, blue `#3F83F8`; hero gradient
  `from-[#4F6BED] to-[#7C3AED]`. Prefer these (or existing theme tokens) over new
  arbitrary colors for accents, tints, glows, and gradient bars.

### Responsiveness (mobile-first, nothing may break)
- Every screen must look native-app-quality from **320px** up through tablet and
  desktop. Nothing may overflow horizontally or touch the viewport edge.
- Horizontal page gutters come from the shared `<main>` (`layout.ts`
  `LAYOUT_MAIN_PADDING_X`); do not add a second `mx-*`/`px-*` gutter on top of it
  inside a page — it double-pads on small screens.
- Control rows must stack (`flex-col … md:flex-row`) rather than keep fixed widths
  that overflow; give inputs `w-full min-w-0 sm:w-[fixed]`. Add `min-w-0` +
  `break-words`/`truncate` to text columns so long titles/URLs can't push layout
  wide. Scope `whitespace-nowrap` to `sm:` and up when the text is long.
- Wide tables/media inside content get an `overflow-x-auto` wrapper.
- Modal/drawer layers must sit **above** the fixed mobile tab bar (`z-[200]`) so
  their panels and CTAs are never clipped (shared overlays already do this).

### Reduced motion & performance
- All motion must be disabled under `prefers-reduced-motion` — the `dash-*` kit
  already handles this; any bespoke animation you add must include a
  `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` off-switch.
- Animate only compositor-friendly properties (`transform`, `opacity`, `filter`);
  never animate layout properties (width/height/top/left) in hot paths. Keep
  durations short (150–550ms) so the UI never feels sluggish.

## Theming (multi-theme — every theme must look great)
The app ships multiple themes (LMS Default + light variants + dark themes) via a
semantic token system in `src/styles.css`. Every new or edited UI element MUST
render correctly in ALL themes — an unreadable/dark-on-dark string or a stray
light "island" in a dark theme is incomplete work, treated like a missing
`data-testid`. Full mapping: `docs/theming-token-map.md`. Visual QA surface: the
`/theme-lab` route. Contrast is enforced in CI by `npm run check:contrast`.
- **Never hardcode a color.** No raw hex (`text-[#111827]`, `bg-[#F9FAFB]`), no
  literal Tailwind color scales (`text-gray-700`, `bg-blue-50`, `border-slate-200`),
  and no fixed `text-white`/`text-black` sitting on a themed fill. Colors come
  from semantic token utilities so they re-theme automatically.
- **Use the semantic tokens** (all wired to Tailwind utilities):
  - Surfaces: `bg-background`, `bg-surface`, `bg-surface-muted`, `bg-card`,
    `bg-popover`, `bg-muted`, `bg-brand-subtle`, `bg-{success,warning,danger,info}-subtle`.
  - Text: `text-foreground`, `text-foreground-muted`, `text-foreground-subtle`,
    `text-brand`, and each fill's `text-*-foreground` / `text-*-subtle-foreground`.
  - Borders/rings: `border-border`, `border-border-strong`, `ring-ring`, `ring-brand`.
  - Brand/accent: `bg-brand`/`text-brand`, `bg-accent-warm`/`hover:bg-accent-warm-hover`;
    status families `success`/`warning`/`danger`/`info`.
- **Colors travel as foreground/background PAIRS** — never choose a text color
  independently of its background. On `bg-brand` use `text-brand-foreground`; on
  `bg-*-subtle` use `text-*-subtle-foreground`. This is what guarantees contrast
  in every theme.
- **Dark themes add a `.dark` class to `<html>`.** For a genuinely decorative
  light background/gradient that can't be tokenized (brand gradient, promo card,
  illustration), KEEP the light classes and ADD `dark:` overrides — never change
  the light classes. e.g. `... from-[#EBF3FE] to-[#F3EDFE] dark:bg-none dark:bg-surface-muted`,
  or `bg-blue-50 text-blue-600 dark:bg-info-subtle dark:text-info-subtle-foreground`.
- **Respect elevation:** raised elements (cards, popovers, dropdowns) use
  `bg-surface`/`bg-card`; recessed/inset fills use `bg-surface-muted`. In dark
  themes elevated surfaces are LIGHTER than the page — don't invert it.
- **Typography color is themed:** `type-*` classes and `MarkdownContent` derive
  their color from `--masai-text-color` (the theme foreground). Do not override
  typography/prose text with a hardcoded color.
- **Adding a theme** = one `[data-theme='<id>']` block in `styles.css` + one
  entry in `src/lib/theme/themes.ts`. Zero component changes.
- **Before shipping UI:** run `npm run check:contrast` (must pass on every
  theme), open `/theme-lab`, and eyeball the feature in Default AND at least one
  dark theme (Midnight/Cocoa).

## Automation Test Hooks
- Add a stable `data-testid` to every meaningful UI element so end-to-end /
  automation suites can target it without depending on copy, styling, or DOM
  structure. Cover: section/container roots, interactive controls
  (buttons, tabs, links, inputs), repeated list items, and key text/status
  elements.
- Use `data-testid` for this — do not add `id`s or class names purely for
  testing, and never key styling off the test id.
- Naming: kebab-case, feature-prefixed — `data-testid="<feature>-<element>"`
  (e.g. `dashboard-welcome-banner`, `dashboard-schedule-card`). For repeated
  list items reuse one id and query all; when a stable domain id exists, suffix
  it (e.g. `dashboard-announcement-item-<id>`).
- Every new UI element (and any UI element you touch) must carry a
  `data-testid`. Do not rename/remove an existing one without updating its
  consumers.

## Analytics / Event Tracking (GA / GTM)
- Every new interactive element (CTA, button, tab, link, card, filter, menu
  item, etc.) must fire a GA/GTM event so nothing ships untracked. Adding a
  clickable without an event is incomplete work — treat it like a missing
  `data-testid`.
- Fire events by pushing to the GTM `dataLayer` via the shared helper
  `pushGtmEvent(event, params)` in `src/utils/gtm.ts`. Do not call
  `window.dataLayer.push` / `window.gtag` directly and do not add new
  ad-hoc helpers.
  - Dashboard surfaces: use `pushDashboardEvent(event, params)` from
    `src/components/features/dashboard/shared/dashboardAnalytics.ts`; every
    dashboard event name must contain the word `dashboard`
    (prefix `l_dashboard_`).
  - Learn surfaces: use `pushLearnEvent(event, params)` /
    `learnEntityEvent(type, action, id)` from
    `src/components/features/learn/shared/learnAnalytics.ts`
    (prefix `l_learn_`).
- Embed the relevant DB row id in the event name (`..._id_<id>`) whenever the
  click targets a specific entity, and use the entity type in the name (e.g.
  `l_learn_lecture_card_click_id_42`, `l_dashboard_fee_payment_cta_id_7`). If
  the id is not already in scope, plumb it through — do not drop it.
- Pass identifying context (ids, entity type, title, tab, page, filter key,
  source surface, etc.) as the `params` object so events are useful for
  segmentation. Fire the event synchronously at click time, before any
  navigation/await, and keep it additive — never change existing
  navigation/mutation behavior.
- Do not track noisy, high-frequency, non-intent signals (e.g. video
  scrubbing, per-keystroke input, volume drags); track the meaningful,
  debounced/committed action instead.

## PR Quality Bar
- Ensure `npm run test` and `npm run lint` pass for touched code.
- Keep changes scoped to the request; do not refactor unrelated areas.
- Add concise code comments only where logic is non-obvious.

## Seed Framework
- Each seed flow under `seed/flows/` must create **its own** users, batch, sections, and lectures.
- Namespace human-readable identifiers by flow id (for example `{flowId}.student@example.com`, `SDE Batch 42 [{flowId}]`).
- Flows must not assume exclusive ownership of shared rows; all flows must work when seeded back-to-back with `--no-reset`.
- Reuse factories from `seed/factories/` and shared builders — never duplicate raw insert logic inside flow seeders.
